{"elapsed_seconds": 10.954334360998473, "event": {"type": "chat.end", "result": {"model_instance_id": "evo-accuracy-qwen35-off-mtp", "output": [{"type": "message", "content": "The author argues that Latin decayed in the East due to practical separation from the West and barbarian devastation [PDF p. 170]. By the seventh century, Latin knowledge was rare, with Justinian being the last emperor to speak it natively [PDF p. 169]. The author attributes this linguistic shift to the division of the empire and the extermination of Latin-speaking populations by Slavs [PDF p. 170]. Simultaneously, the empire became increasingly Christian [PDF p. 171]. The author notes that Christian influence on state machinery began slowly, accelerating under Theodosius I [PDF p. 171]. The author highlights that gladiatorial games ended after a monk\u2019s death, not state decree [PDF p. 171]. Christianity also abolished infanticide and modified slavery by emphasizing the soul\u2019s equality [PDF p. 173]. The author credits these social improvements to the Christian belief in individual worth [PDF p. 174]. However, the author explicitly evaluates the rise of monasticism as dangerous [PDF p. 174]. The author claims monks neglected civic duties, harming the state during barbarian invasions [PDF p. 175]. The author describes this apathy as maddening to pagans [PDF p. 176]. The author concludes that governing classes preserved Roman traditions despite this religious shift [PDF p. 176]. The author observes that paganism vanished, though indifference persisted among educated Christians [PDF p. 176]. The author suggests that while Christianity improved social conditions, its ascetic elements posed significant political risks to the empire\u2019s stability [PDF p. 174]."}], "stats": {"input_tokens": 3630, "total_output_tokens": 359, "reasoning_output_tokens": 0, "tokens_per_second": 66.3680721372744, "time_to_first_token_seconds": 4.682692}}}}
